Source: the crates.io quinn-proto 0.11.19 package, without its generated lockfile
or registry cache markers. The upstream MIT and Apache-2.0 licenses are retained.
Upstream repository: https://github.com/quinn-rs/quinn
Crates.io package checksum:
0e750cca55fe4f0439a15d0bb529da9651e79993e8e72c61a899a36d462befbe

Local changes: src/connection/mod.rs limits pad_to_mtu to ACK-eliciting packets;
src/tests/mod.rs adds a deterministic regression that decodes the frames in a
padded peer's response to PING and requires a pure ACK without PADDING.
ACK-only packets padded by upstream consume congestion credit without eliciting
an acknowledgement or arming PTO after address validation. Large DNS requests
can then leave the server unable to transmit its response. This patch retains
QUIC packet padding for application data without padding pure acknowledgements.
Loss-probe padding exceptions and QUIC congestion control remain upstream code.

The root Cargo.toml applies this patch only to the existing quinn-proto package.
The vendored crate is excluded from workspace membership. No dependency version
or cryptographic implementation is changed.

Regression coverage lives in crates/transport/src/doq/tests/:
- padding.rs observes padded 1-RTT packets in both directions, including real
  TSIG transactions and messages without EDNS.
- protocol.rs exchanges maximum-length DNS messages on a reused connection.
- transfers.rs covers concurrent AXFR/IXFR streams and transfer limits.

Run the upstream protocol engine tests and the deterministic regression with:
cargo test --manifest-path vendor/quinn-proto/Cargo.toml --no-default-features --features rustls-ring --lib

When upgrading Quinn, compare the upstream padding implementation with this
change and rerun those regressions before removing or updating the patch.
